---
description: Test architecture and code organization guidelines
alwaysApply: true
---

# Architecture

- Follow the existing project architecture.
- Use Page Object Model (POM) for reusable page-level interactions.
- Keep business assertions in test files whenever practical.
- Page objects should encapsulate page interactions and locators.
- Do not turn page objects into large collections of unrelated methods.
- Keep API clients and business logic separate from UI page objects.
- Use fixtures for reusable setup, authentication, and test dependencies.

# Fixtures

- Prefer Playwright fixtures over beforeEach/afterEach
  for reusable test setup.
- Use test-scoped fixtures by default.
- Use worker-scoped fixtures only for immutable or safely shared resources.
- Ensure fixtures clean up resources they create.
- Avoid hidden dependencies between fixtures.
- Keep fixture initialization predictable and explicit.

# Test data

- Do not hardcode reusable test data throughout test files.
- Use factories or dedicated test-data builders for complex entities.
- Generate unique data when tests can run in parallel.
- Avoid relying on pre-existing mutable application state.
- Keep test data deterministic wherever possible.

# Code quality

- Avoid duplicated selectors and interaction logic.
- Avoid overly generic helper methods.
- Prefer explicit method names describing user actions.
- Keep methods small and focused.
- Avoid excessive inheritance.
- Do not create abstractions for one-time operations.